\subsection{Sensitivity to True Lag and Noise Level}

Figure~\ref{fig:combineduncertainty} and Tables~\ref{tab:lagsensitivity} and~\ref{tab:noisesensitivity} show that fractional lag uncertainty decreases monotonically with satellite count for all three true lags and is larger for shorter lags. For the 5-day lag the mean recovered lags (@@S_L5_LAG3@@ days at 3 satellites, @@S_L5_LAG12@@ days at 12) exceed the true lag, so the fractional errors there are normalized by a biased lag. At every noise level 12 satellites give a lower uncertainty than 3, but the benefit shrinks as noise grows: at 6\% noise the step from 6 to 12 satellites is small (@@S_N6_6@@ to @@S_N12_6@@), and at 10\% noise it vanishes (@@S_N6_10@@ and @@S_N12_10@@). The dependence on noise is not strictly monotonic for 3 satellites (@@S_N3@@, @@S_N3_6@@, @@S_N3_10@@ at 3\%, 6\% and 10\%) because, at high noise, the correlation is weak and the centroid is biased toward long lags (mean recovered lags of @@S_N_LAG3_6@@ and @@S_N_LAG3_10@@ days at 6\% and 10\%), which deflates a fractional error normalized by the recovered lag.

