\subsection{Astrophysical Parameter Sweeps}
\label{sec:sweeps}

Table~\ref{tab:sweeps} and Figure~\ref{fig:sweeps} vary the DRW damping timescale, the variability amplitude and the transfer function in the controlled experiment, with and without a 36-day seasonal gap. Four results stand out. First, uncertainties grow with $\tau_d$: for 3 satellites with uniform sampling the fractional error rises from @@SW_tau_20_uniform_3@@ at 20 days to @@SW_base_uniform_3@@ at 50 days and @@SW_tau_200_uniform_3@@ at 200 days, because slowly varying light curves contain less information on the lag timescale. Second, low-amplitude AGN are the hardest: at 1\% amplitude the uncertainty stays near @@SW_amp_0p01_uniform_12@@ even with 12 satellites and the recovered lag (@@SWL_amp_0p01_uniform_12@@ days) is far from the true 10 days, i.e.\ lag recovery fails, whereas at 10\% and 15\% amplitude it falls to @@SW_amp_0p10_uniform_12@@ and @@SW_amp_0p15_uniform_12@@. Third, skewed transfer functions raise the uncertainty and shift the recovered lag away from the true mean lag (for the log-normal with $\sigma = 4$~days the uncertainties are @@SW_transfer_lognormal_4_uniform_3@@, @@SW_transfer_lognormal_4_uniform_6@@ and @@SW_transfer_lognormal_4_uniform_12@@, with mean recovered lags of @@SWL_transfer_lognormal_4_uniform_3@@, @@SWL_transfer_lognormal_4_uniform_6@@ and @@SWL_transfer_lognormal_4_uniform_12@@ days for 3, 6 and 12 satellites, against a true mean lag of 10 days). Fourth, the seasonal gap raises the uncertainty everywhere and removes most of the benefit of additional satellites: in the fiducial case the 12-satellite uncertainty rises from @@SW_base_uniform_12@@ to @@SW_base_gap_12@@, and the gain from 3 to 12 satellites falls from @@SW_base_uniform_3@@--@@SW_base_uniform_12@@ to @@SW_base_gap_3@@--@@SW_base_gap_12@@, with recovered lags of @@SWL_base_gap_3@@, @@SWL_base_gap_6@@ and @@SWL_base_gap_12@@ days. In relative terms the gap is most damaging for short damping timescales ($\tau_d = 20$~days: @@SW_tau_20_uniform_12@@ with uniform sampling versus @@SW_tau_20_gap_12@@ with a gap, for 12 satellites), consistent with aliasing of faster variability across the gap.

