With a cycle time of @@CYCLE_S@@~s, a satellite could make 480 exposures per day if it could observe continuously, but Earth occultation, eclipses and (for some targets) the Sun limit the usable time, and the single station is in view for only about @@PASS_MIN@@ minutes per day. For NGC~5548 a single satellite is limited by observing time to about @@DL1_TIME@@ exposures per day, and downlink is not binding for photometric windows: 64~kB per exposure allows @@DL1_CUT@@ exposures per day for one satellite and @@DL12_CUT@@ for twelve (Table~\ref{tab:downlink}). Downlinking full frames, however, limits a single satellite to @@DL1_FULL@@ exposures per day and twelve satellites to @@DL12_FULL@@, so the readout strategy can matter as much as the number of satellites. In all cases the achievable per-satellite cadence is much finer than the 2-day placeholder of the controlled experiment, which therefore understates what a single satellite can do.

