\subsection{Dynamic Orbits and Catalogued Targets}
\label{sec:targets}

\textbf{Solar exclusion.} Table~\ref{tab:targets} and Figure~\ref{fig:targets}a show how many of 120 days fall inside the $45^{\circ}$ Sun cone. Fairall~9 (ecliptic latitude @@TG_Fairall9_LAT@@) is never excluded, the mid-latitude targets NGC~5548, NGC~4151 and NGC~3783 lose up to @@TG_NGC5548_EXMAX@@ days, and the low-latitude targets NGC~7469 and Mrk~509 lose up to @@TG_NGC7469_EXMAX@@ and @@TG_Mrk509_EXMAX@@ days, with a longest single gap of @@TG_Mrk509_GAP@@ days. The excluded time depends strongly on when the campaign starts, from zero for a well-placed start to these maxima, so for low-latitude targets the campaign must be timed or extended over more than one season.

\textbf{Occultation and coverage.} With the precessing orbit, at least one satellite always has an unobstructed view of the target (Earth occultation alone leaves @@OCC_ALL@@ of the grid observable for every target and every constellation of 3 or more satellites), because the occulted fraction of an orbit is under half. For a campaign starting on 1 January 2026, the fraction of 0.5-day bins containing data is @@MI_COV1@@ for NGC~5548 with one satellite and @@MI_COV12@@ with twelve, but only @@TG_NGC7469_COV@@ for NGC~7469 and @@TG_Mrk509_COV@@ for Mrk~509 because their Sun gaps cover most of the campaign.

\textbf{Lag uncertainty.} At $V = 15$ and 4\% variability, a single satellite already reaches @@TG_NGC5548_E1@@ for NGC~5548, @@TG_NGC3783_E1@@ for NGC~3783 and @@TG_Fairall9_E1@@ for Fairall~9, and twelve satellites reach @@TG_NGC5548_E12@@, @@TG_NGC3783_E12@@ and @@TG_Fairall9_E12@@. The difference between constellation sizes is therefore modest, a few percentage points that are partly within the statistical noise of 30 realizations. For the low-latitude targets the uncertainty is far larger (@@TG_NGC7469_E1@@ and @@TG_Mrk509_E1@@ with one satellite, @@TG_NGC7469_E12@@ and @@TG_Mrk509_E12@@ with twelve), and the recovered lag for Mrk~509 is biased high (@@TG_Mrk509_LAG1@@ days with one satellite and @@TG_Mrk509_LAG12@@ days with twelve). Adding satellites reduces these uncertainties, but they remain large, whereas changing the campaign start date removes the gap altogether.

