\textbf{Brightness and variability.} Table~\ref{tab:mission} and Figure~\ref{fig:mission} show when constellation size matters. When photons are scarce the benefit is large: at $V = 17$ and 4\% variability the fractional lag uncertainty falls from @@MI_V17_A04_E1@@ with one satellite to @@MI_V17_A04_E3@@, @@MI_V17_A04_E6@@ and @@MI_V17_A04_E12@@ with 3, 6 and 12. The same holds for weakly variable AGN: at $V = 15$ and 1\% variability it falls from @@MI_V15_A01_E1@@ to @@MI_V15_A01_E12@@. For a bright target with strong variability ($V = 15$, 10\%) the uncertainty is already @@MI_V15_A10_E1@@ with one satellite and does not improve with more (@@MI_V15_A10_E12@@ with twelve). The constellation size therefore matters when the lag signal is limited by photon noise, and not when it is limited by the AGN's own variability and by the observing window.

