\section{Discussion}

In the controlled experiment, increasing the constellation from 3 to 12 satellites reduces the fractional lag uncertainty from @@B3_FL@@ to @@B12_FL@@, consistent with the hypothesis, but the pattern is not the one the hypothesis expected. The absolute gain shrinks, yet the uncertainty falls roughly as $N^{-0.65}$ with no plateau, even though the 3-satellite cadence is already about 15 times finer than the 10-day lag, and the binning test shows that the improvement comes from averaging photometric noise over more epochs and not from a finer sampling interval. The field's rule of thumb that cadence finer than about five times the lag brings diminishing returns (Shablovinskaya et al., 2023) is therefore not what limits the precision here; what limits it is the number of photons collected.

This reading is reinforced by the mission model, in which noise and cadence are derived. A single 10-cm satellite already collects about @@MI_EXPO1@@ exposures per day for a mid-latitude target, far more than the 0.5 epochs per day implied by the 2-day placeholder, so the question changes from ``how fine can the cadence be?'' to ``how many photons does the lag measurement need?''. Constellation size helps when the photometry is the bottleneck (faint targets, low variability amplitude, or a narrow line channel) and does little when the intrinsic variability or the observing window is the bottleneck. In those cases a larger aperture, longer exposures or a different readout strategy may give the same improvement as additional satellites, and a constellation also adds redundancy, which is a separate benefit not quantified here.

The seasonal gap is the dominant limitation for low-ecliptic-latitude targets. It biases the recovered lag toward larger values, inflates the uncertainty, aliases fast variability, and removes most of the benefit of additional satellites, because the gap is the same for every satellite. High-ecliptic-latitude targets avoid it, and for targets within a few tens of degrees of the ecliptic the campaign start must be chosen, or the campaign extended over more than one season, to avoid it. This supports using target selection and campaign timing as design variables on a par with constellation size, and echoes the finding of Malik et al.\ (2022) and Malik (2023) that the observing window can matter more than cadence.

The sweeps show how far the results depend on the AGN: short damping timescales and large amplitudes are easier, low-amplitude AGN may not give a recoverable lag at all with 3\% noise and a 120-day baseline, and skewed transfer functions shift the centroid lag below the true mean lag. The reported percentages should therefore not be applied to a specific AGN without simulating its variability. The estimator diagnostics add that the all-lags centroid is conservative in its uncertainties but biased toward long lags when the correlation is weak, and that its uncertainties depend on the centroid definition.

Several limitations apply. The instrument parameters, ground station, downlink rate, data volume per exposure, thermal recovery time and Sun-avoidance angle are representative assumptions and not a specific design; the orbit is a single circular, 51.6$^{\circ}$ inclination orbit with one initial node, and we do not model Earth-limb avoidance, slewing between targets, pointing stability, radiation-belt passages or the South Atlantic Anomaly. All targets share one magnitude, one damping timescale and one transfer-function family per run, the line channel is treated without continuum contamination, and the 20\% velocity floor and the fixed virial factor mean that the mass uncertainties are statistical only (the systematic uncertainty in $f$, typically a few tenths of a dex, would exceed the differences between configurations). Finally, only the ICCF estimator was used. Methods that model the continuum as a Gaussian process, such as JAVELIN (Zu et al., 2011) and PyROA (Donnan et al., 2021), are standard in the field and generally handle irregular sampling and gaps better than linear interpolation; running them on these simulated light curves (the code repository provides an export tool) is the most important extension, because the conclusions about gaps and about faint, low-variability AGN may change with a better estimator.

\section{Conclusion}

We tested how CubeSat constellation size affects the uncertainty of reverberation-mapped lags and black hole masses for bright AGN with BLR lags of days to weeks. In a controlled experiment, increasing the constellation from 3 to 12 satellites reduced fractional lag uncertainty from @@B3_FL@@ to @@B12_FL@@ and mass uncertainty from @@B3_FM@@ to @@B12_FM@@ ($p<0.001$ for all pairwise comparisons); the uncertainty fell roughly as $N^{-0.65}$ without a plateau, and a binning test showed that the gain comes from averaging photometric noise over more epochs and not from finer sampling. In a mission-level model with a photon budget, a precessing orbit, a real solar ephemeris and six catalogued AGN, a single satellite already collects many exposures per day, and constellation size matters mainly for faint or weakly variable targets (for $V = 17$ the uncertainty falls from @@MI_V17_A04_E1@@ with one satellite to @@MI_V17_A04_E12@@ with twelve), not for bright, strongly varying ones. Solar exclusion depends on ecliptic latitude, from no exclusion at high latitude to gaps of up to @@TG_Mrk509_GAP@@ days near the ecliptic, and a seasonal gap biases the lag and removes most of the benefit of additional satellites. A low-cost constellation could therefore expand the set of AGN accessible to reverberation mapping, but target selection, campaign timing, aperture and data strategy matter at least as much as the number of satellites. Future work should compare ICCF with Gaussian-process estimators such as JAVELIN and PyROA, vary the orbit orientation and campaign start in the mission model, treat the line channel with continuum contamination, use measured magnitudes and variability properties for each target, and evaluate masses with a luminosity-calibrated radius--luminosity relation.

